    "stop_reason": "success",
    "result_text": "Bulk open is working: 3 of the 4 tests in `e2e/specs/acceptance.spec.ts` pass with `bin/e2e`, including the main \"bulk open\" test with all its numbers and audit lines. **\"bulk open scenario 2\" still fails**, at a person-edit step that runs before any bulk open (details at the end).\n\n**How it works**\n- **Checkboxes:** each claim row now has a `claim-select` checkbox (`person-claims.ts`). The selection is kept in `ClaimsService`, and claims that disappear after a re-load drop out of it.\n- **Button and result:** next to the export button there is now an \"Open selected claims\" button (`bulk-open`). Afterwards it shows how many were opened (`bulk-opened`) and refused (`bulk-refused`), plus a `bulk-error` message if the request itself fails. It then re-reads claims and people and clears the selection.\n- **Server:** a new `POST /api/sheets/{id}/claims/bulk-open` in `ClaimController` calls a new `ClaimService.bulkOpen`. This sorts the chosen claims and opens each one through the existing open logic. So every open gets the same checks and the same audit lines as the single Open button (`CLAIM_OPENED`, then `ROLLUP` and `TOTALS`). A claim that can't be opened counts as refused and is left unchanged. Unknown ids also count as refused.\n- **One transaction per claim:** I first ran the whole bulk open as one transaction, but one refusal (e.g. a claim in a closed period) rolled back all of it. Each claim is now opened in its own transaction, so a refusal undoes only that claim.\n\n**Numbering order differs from the request.** The request says newest claim first within a person, but the test expects the oldest first: Ada's 10.00 claim gets number 2 and her later 20.00 claim gets 3. I followed the test, so claims are numbered in the people-list order, then oldest first within each person. If the request's wording is what you actually want, it's a one-line change in `ClaimService.bulkOpen`, but this test would then fail.\n\n**Why scenario 2 fails:** at spec line 356 it edits Cam to grade C from 2026-02-10. But Cam already has grade B from today (2026-04-11), so the row still shows B as the grade in force today. The test's helper waits for the row to show \"C\", which never happens, so it times out. The test's own expected audit line for that edit says `grade=B` while the row is supposed to show C, so the test contradicts itself; the existing app writes the grade that was asked for into the audit line. This is the existing dated-grades behaviour, not the bulk-open code, so I haven't changed it.",
